Chapter: NicRecv
  cites Kernel chapter Ne2k

 Receive a frame through the driver, not by hand.

 nic-ping covers the neighbouring ground and cannot cover this: it drives
 the NE2000 data port a byte at a time with port-in-byte, so it never
 issues a string operation and never reaches net-recv-raw. That left the
 driver's own receive path -- ne2k-recv-frame, net-recv-raw, and the
 REP INSW the emitter writes for it -- exercised by no test in the
 battery, which is how a change to the host's handling of that
 instruction came to be made against no coverage at all.

 This sends one ARP request through ne2k-send-frame and drains the reply
 through ne2k-recv-frame. codex-vm's NAT answers ARP for the gateway, the
 same reply nic-ping already depends on.

Section: Frame

  arp-for : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  arp-for (m0) (m1) (m2) (m3) (m4) (m5) =
   [255, 255, 255, 255, 255, 255,
    m0, m1, m2, m3, m4, m5,
    8, 6,
    0, 1, 8, 0, 6, 4, 0, 1,
    m0, m1, m2, m3, m4, m5,
    10, 0, 2, 15,
    0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0,
    10, 0, 2, 2]

Section: Drain

 The fuel cap is the failure and not the success. A probe that gave up
 early would report nothing received and read exactly like a working NIC
 with nothing to say, so running out of fuel has to be visible in the
 output rather than indistinguishable from a quiet wire.

  drain : Integer -> [Network.Read] List Integer
  drain (fuel) = act
    if fuel <= 0 then []
    else act
      f <- ne2k-recv-frame
      if list-length f > 0 then f
      else drain (fuel - 1)
    end
  end

Section: Body

 The length alone would not test the transfer. net-recv-raw takes it from
 the four header bytes it reads with ordinary port reads, so a receive
 that moved the body as garbage -- or moved none of it -- still answers a
 plausible length and still looks like an arriving frame. What separates
 those is the CONTENT, so this reads back three fields the gateway's
 reply is obliged to carry: the ARP ethertype at 12, the reply opcode at
 20, and the gateway's own address at 28.

  at : List Integer, Integer -> Integer
  at (f) (i) = if i >= list-length f then 0 - 1 else list-at f i

  opening : [Console, Network.Read, Network.Write] Nothing = act
    m0 <- net-get-hwaddr 0
    m1 <- net-get-hwaddr 1
    m2 <- net-get-hwaddr 2
    m3 <- net-get-hwaddr 3
    m4 <- net-get-hwaddr 4
    m5 <- net-get-hwaddr 5
    sent <- ne2k-send-frame (arp-for m0 m1 m2 m3 m4 m5)
    f <- drain 200000
    print-line-uni ("sent=" & show sent)
    print-line-uni ("len=" & show (list-length f))
    print-line-uni ("ethertype=" & show (at f 12) & " " & show (at f 13))
    print-line-uni ("op=" & show (at f 20) & " " & show (at f 21))
    print-line-uni ("sender-ip=" & show (at f 28) & " " & show (at f 29) & " " & show (at f 30) & " " & show (at f 31))
  end

